The rules (a negative price, a morning hour, a switch-on and a switch-off line
as factors of the day's average, otherwise keep) come from the owner's own
cron script for the same boiler. The default factors come from replaying that
script's relay log against the real prices of the same days.

The Shelly client (shelly_control/shelly.py), its send-time allowlist check,
the fake device in tests/ and the CI guard are adapted from shelly-bridge
(https://github.com/Yornik/shelly-bridge), by the same author, under the MIT
License. The RPC methods, reply fields and the authentication scheme come from
Shelly's public Gen2 API documentation (https://shelly-api-docs.shelly.cloud/gen2/).
The price list format comes from tibber-exporter
(https://github.com/Yornik/tibber-exporter). No code was copied from Shelly's
documentation. The prices in tests/fixtures/ are public electricity prices of
20 to 24 October 2026, without any account or home identifier; every other
test value is made up.

